On 10/23/05 10:13 AM, Doug Freyburger wrote:

> Consider how bad a worm tooth is as a weapon, and
> then consider that it has secondary functions.  If
> must suck at those secondary functions.  View that
> worm tooth as a tin opener and it does suck at it.

Uh, yes, mainly because it isn't considered a tin opener at all. You're 
opening it with your bare hands. See food-343.txt. A crysknife, on the 
other hand, is as good as a dagger; on which, see below.

> Weapons and tools that function as both do have some
> correlation as to how good they are.  Daggers may
> be wonderfull as missiles, but there are very many
> better melee weapons.  Sure enough they do poorly
> as tin openers. 

They're a second-best option, opening tins in three moves while a tin 
opener takes one. Still, they're better than axes or pick-axes, and much 
better than opening by hand. I wouldn't say they do poorly; they're 
useful enough that I never bother using a tin opener.

> Bull whip is a poor weapon and only rarely usefull as a tool.

But a rather cool tool, if you can snag a good weapon away from a 
hostile monster.

> Pick axe is near the middle among weaon qualities and as a tool it is
> near the middle on usefullness.

Depends on how much digging you want to do. Like most tools (in NH and 
in RL), they're pointless until you want to do what they're designed 
for; then they're nearly indispensable.

> The pattern is not exact and it is open to interpretation, but the 
> pattern is there.

Well, in the very broad sense that some things are more useful than 
others, some things have magical properties while others don't. I think 
the devteam are thinking "What could a dagger do aside from being a 
weapon?" more than "Let's range weapon and tool usefulness on a scale 
and make them somewhat equivalent for each individual item."

